"""
Health & Safety Question Bank: exactly 34 Easy, 33 Medium, 33 Hard = 100 questions.
Focus: Occupational Health & Safety (OHS), Hierarchy of Controls, Fire Safety, LOTO, Confined Space, First Aid & CPR, and Safety Codes.
"""

hs_easy = [
    ("In occupational health and safety, what is the fundamental difference between a 'Hazard' and a 'Risk'?", "A hazard is a potential source of harm; a risk is the likelihood and severity of harm occurring from that hazard", ["A hazard is an insured financial loss; a risk is a legal criminal penalty", "A hazard applies to machinery; a risk applies only to human beings", "There is zero difference; both terms mean the exact same thing"], "A hazard is an inherent danger (e.g., electricity, toxic gas); risk combines the probability of exposure with consequence severity."),
    ("In the universal 'Hierarchy of Controls', what is the most effective method to control an occupational hazard?", "Elimination (physically removing the hazard completely)", ["Substitution", "Engineering controls", "Personal Protective Equipment (PPE)"], "Eliminating the hazard entirely (e.g., eliminating manual lifting using automated conveyors) completely eradicates the risk at source."),
    ("In the Hierarchy of Controls, what is considered the LEAST effective, last line of defense?", "Personal Protective Equipment (PPE) (e.g., gloves, masks, earplugs, hard hats)", ["Administrative controls", "Engineering controls", "Substitution"], "PPE is the least reliable because it does not remove the hazard and relies entirely on human compliance, proper fit, and maintenance."),
    ("What are the four components of the 'Fire Tetrahedron' required to sustain a fire?", "Heat, Fuel, Oxygen, and an Uninhibited Chemical Chain Reaction", ["Water, Carbon, Smoke, and Nitrogen", "Electricity, Friction, Gas, and Ash", "Wood, Matches, Wind, and Coal"], "Extinguishing a fire requires removing at least one element: cooling (heat), smothering (oxygen), starving (fuel), or interrupting chain reaction."),
    ("In fire safety classification, what does a 'Class A' fire involve?", "Ordinary solid combustible materials such as wood, paper, cloth, trash, and plastics", ["Flammable liquids such as petrol, diesel, paints, and solvents", "Flammable gases such as LPG, CNG, and methane", "Combustible metals such as magnesium, sodium, and titanium"], "Class A fires leave ash and are quenched effectively by cooling with water or multi-purpose dry chemical powder."),
    ("In fire classification, what does a 'Class B' fire involve?", "Flammable liquids and liquefiable solids (e.g., petrol, oil, kerosene, paints, and grease)", ["Ordinary paper and dry cardboard boxes", "Energized electrical transformers and circuits", "Deep commercial kitchen cooking oils and fats"], "Class B fires require smothering agents (foam, CO2, dry powder) that cut off oxygen without splashing burning liquid."),
    ("In fire classification, what does a 'Class C' fire involve in standard international/Indian systems?", "Flammable gases (e.g., LPG, propane, butane, hydrogen, natural gas)", ["Ordinary wood and tree branches", "Combustible metals like lithium and potassium", "Kitchen vegetable cooking oil deep fryers"], "Class C gas fires require isolating the gas fuel supply valve first before extinguishing the flame to prevent gas explosion."),
    ("In fire classification, what does a 'Class D' fire involve?", "Combustible metals (e.g., magnesium, titanium, zirconium, sodium, and potassium)", ["Paper and cardboard office waste", "Motor oil and automotive gasoline", "Electrical computer servers"], "Metal fires burn at extreme temperatures and react violently with water; they require specialized Class D dry powder flux agents."),
    ("In fire safety, what type of extinguisher should NEVER be used on energized electrical fires?", "Water extinguisher", ["Carbon Dioxide (CO2) extinguisher", "Dry Chemical Powder (DCP) extinguisher", "Clean Agent Halon replacement"], "Water conducts electricity, posing severe electrocution and shock hazards to the firefighter."),
    ("What does the 'PASS' acronym stand for when operating a portable fire extinguisher?", "Pull the pin, Aim the nozzle at the base of the fire, Squeeze the lever, and Sweep side to side", ["Push the button, Alert the team, Stand back, and Spray", "Prepare the hose, Activate the valve, Secure the exit, and Stop", "Point at flames, Assess danger, Shout fire, and Scramble"], "PASS guides rapid, effective extinguisher operation: aiming at the burning fuel base extinguishes flames efficiently."),
    ("What does 'LOTO' stand for in industrial machinery maintenance safety?", "Lockout / Tagout (isolating hazardous energy sources and locking/tagging switches during servicing)", ["Logistics Operations and Transport Organization", "Light Optimization and Temperature Order", "Live Overhaul and Testing Output"], "LOTO prevents accidental machine startup or release of stored kinetic, electrical, or hydraulic energy while technicians work."),
    ("What is 'Personal Protective Equipment' (PPE)?", "Equipment, garments, or gear worn by workers to minimize exposure to workplace hazards causing injuries or illnesses", ["Standard casual civilian clothing worn to work", "The corporate identity badge worn on office shirts", "A medical prescription tablet prescribed by a doctor"], "PPE includes safety helmets, steel-toed boots, goggles, respirators, high-visibility vests, and fall arrest harnesses."),
    ("What head protection PPE is mandatory on construction sites and heavy industrial plants?", "Safety Helmet (Hard Hat)", ["Woolen winter cap", "Baseball cap", "Chef's kitchen hat"], "Hard hats protect the cranium from falling tools, structural impacts, and incidental contact with electrical conductors."),
    ("What does a 'Safety Harness' protect a worker against when working at heights?", "Arresting a fall and preventing impact injury from falling from elevated platforms, roofs, or scaffolding", ["Protecting against chemical inhalation fumes", "Protecting the eyes from flying welding sparks", "Keeping the body warm during cold winter weather"], "Full-body harnesses distribute fall arrest forces across thighs, pelvis, chest, and shoulders when linked to a secure anchor."),
    ("In workplace first aid, what does 'CPR' stand for?", "Cardiopulmonary Resuscitation", ["Central Patient Rehabilitation", "Cardiac Pulse Recovery", "Certified Paramedic Response"], "CPR is an emergency lifesaving procedure combining chest compressions and artificial breaths to maintain vital organ perfusion."),
    ("In adult CPR, what is the recommended chest compression rate per minute according to international resuscitation guidelines?", "100 to 120 compressions per minute", ["50 to 60 compressions per minute", "180 to 200 compressions per minute", "Exactly 30 compressions per minute"], "Pumping at 100-120 bpm (to the beat of 'Stayin' Alive') maintains optimal artificial cardiac output during cardiac arrest."),
    ("In standard adult CPR performed by trained responders, what is the ratio of chest compressions to rescue breaths?", "30 chest compressions followed by 2 rescue breaths (30:2 ratio)", ["15 compressions to 5 breaths", "50 compressions to 1 breath", "10 compressions to 10 breaths"], "The 30:2 cycle prioritizes continuous blood circulation while delivering supplementary oxygenation to vital brain cells."),
    ("What does an 'AED' stand for in sudden cardiac arrest emergency response?", "Automated External Defibrillator", ["Advanced Emergency Diagnostic", "Automated Electrical Disconnect", "Ambulance Emergency Dispatcher"], "An AED is a portable electronic device that analyzes heart rhythm and delivers an electrical shock to treat ventricular fibrillation."),
    ("What first aid maneuver is used to clear a severe foreign body airway obstruction in a conscious choking person?", "Heimlich Maneuver (Abdominal Thrusts)", ["Mouth-to-mouth rescue breathing", "Laying the person flat on their back", "Giving the person a glass of cold water"], "Quick inward and upward abdominal thrusts compress the diaphragm, using trapped lung air to expel the lodged foreign object."),
    ("What is the immediate first aid treatment for a minor thermal heat burn (first-degree burn)?", "Cool the burn under cold running tap water for at least 10 to 20 minutes", ["Apply butter, cooking oil, or toothpaste over the burn", "Pop any blisters that form using a sewing needle", "Pack the burn tightly with frozen ice cubes"], "Cool running water dissipates heat, halts deep tissue cooking, reduces pain, and minimizes swelling and blistering."),
    ("What is a 'Confined Space' in industrial workplace safety?", "An enclosed space with restricted entry/exit, not designed for continuous human occupancy, and prone to atmospheric hazards", ["A small private office cubicle with a door", "An executive conference boardroom during meetings", "A passenger elevator operating in a hospital"], "Confined spaces (tanks, silos, manholes, sewers, ducts) carry acute risks of toxic gases, flammable vapors, and oxygen deficiency."),
    ("What is the safe normal atmospheric oxygen concentration range required for human breathing inside a confined space?", "19.5% to 23.5% Oxygen by volume", ["10.0% to 15.0% Oxygen", "25.0% to 35.0% Oxygen", "Above 50.0% Oxygen"], "Oxygen below 19.5% causes asphyxiation and unconsciousness; oxygen above 23.5% creates an extreme fire/explosion hazard."),
    ("What does 'SDS' (or MSDS) stand for in chemical hazard communication?", "Safety Data Sheet (Material Safety Data Sheet)", ["Standard Disinfection Schedule", "Systemic Decontamination Standard", "Safe Disposal System"], "An SDS is a standardized 16-section technical document detailing chemical hazards, handling, storage, PPE, and first aid."),
    ("In chemical safety, what color and pictogram does GHS use to indicate explosive, flammable, or toxic chemical hazards?", "Red diamond border with a black symbol on a white background", ["Green circle with a white cross", "Blue square with a yellow triangle", "Purple rectangle with black stars"], "The standardized GHS hazard pictograms ensure universal, language-independent visual recognition of chemical dangers."),
    ("What does 'ELCB' or 'RCCB' stand for in electrical installation safety?", "Earth Leakage Circuit Breaker / Residual Current Circuit Breaker", ["Electronic Line Control Board", "Emergency Lighting Current Battery", "Electrical Low Consumption Box"], "RCCBs detect tiny leakage currents to ground (e.g., 30 mA) and trip power in milliseconds, preventing lethal electrocution."),
    ("What is 'Ergonomics' in occupational health and safety?", "The science of fitting workplace conditions, furniture, and job demands to the physical and cognitive capabilities of the worker", ["The mathematical calculation of corporate income tax", "The decorative painting of factory building exteriors", "The training of employees in foreign spoken languages"], "Ergonomic design prevents Musculoskeletal Disorders (MSDs), repetitive strain injuries (RSI), carpal tunnel syndrome, and back pain."),
    ("What is the recommended safe lifting posture when lifting a heavy box from the floor?", "Bend your knees, keep your back straight, hold the load close to your body, and lift with your legs", ["Keep your legs completely straight, bend your spine, and jerk upward", "Twist your torso sideways while lifting the heavy weight", "Hold the heavy load at arm's length away from your chest"], "Lifting with leg quadriceps keeps the spine aligned, preventing severe lumbar disc herniation and muscle strains."),
    ("At what continuous noise exposure level is hearing protection (earplugs or earmuffs) mandatorily required under OHS standards?", "85 decibels (dBA) over an 8-hour Time-Weighted Average (TWA)", ["50 decibels (dBA)", "120 decibels (dBA)", "30 decibels (dBA)"], "Prolonged exposure above 85 dBA permanently damages delicate cochlear hair cells, causing irreversible noise-induced hearing loss."),
    ("In industrial facilities, what does an 'Emergency Eye Wash Station' provide?", "A dedicated plumbed fixture providing immediate, continuous flushing water for eyes exposed to hazardous chemicals", ["A mirror for workers to inspect their eyeglasses", "A station where employees take routine eye vision exams", "A storage cabinet for clean reading glasses"], "Emergency eyewash stations must provide 15 minutes of continuous flushing to wash corrosive chemicals out before blindness occurs."),
    ("What is an 'Assembly Point' (Muster Point) during a building fire evacuation?", "A designated safe outdoor area away from the building where all evacuees gather for headcounts and roll call", ["The central cafeteria inside the ground floor of the building", "The underground vehicle parking garage basement", "The rooftop helipad located at the top of the skyscraper"], "Assembly points ensure everyone is accounted for and prevent panicked individuals from wandering back into burning structures."),
    ("What is a 'Near Miss' in workplace safety incident reporting?", "An unplanned event that did NOT result in injury, illness, or damage, but had the potential to do so under slightly different circumstances", ["An accident that results in permanent human fatality", "A worker arriving at the office five minutes late", "A product that fails quality inspection in a warehouse"], "Near misses are free safety warnings: investigating near misses and fixing root causes prevents catastrophic accidents later."),
    ("What does 'Heinrich's Safety Triangle' (Heinrich's Law) illustrate regarding incident ratios?", "For every 1 major injury, there are 29 minor injuries and 300 non-injury near-miss incidents", ["For every 10 managers, there are 100 workers and 1,000 machines", "For every 1 dollar spent on safety, company profits double", "Accidents happen only on Fridays at the end of shifts"], "H.W. Heinrich showed that reducing the broad base of unsafe behaviors and near misses prevents the apex catastrophic fatalities."),
    ("In industrial facilities, what is the purpose of 'Toolbox Talks' (Safety Briefings)?", "Short, informal 5- to 10-minute safety discussions held at the job site prior to starting daily work shifts", ["A formal legal negotiation conducted with labor trade unions", "An annual lecture delivered by the company chief executive", "A training course teaching workers how to polish metal hand tools"], "Toolbox talks refresh hazard awareness, review task-specific risks, check PPE, and align daily safety focus on the ground."),
    ("Under the Factories Act, 1948 in India, what is the maximum permissible daily working hours for an adult worker?", "9 hours per day (and not more than 48 hours per week)", ["12 hours per day", "6 hours per day", "15 hours per day"], "Section 51 and 54 establish that adult workers cannot be required or allowed to work more than 9 hours daily or 48 hours weekly.")
]

hs_med = [
    ("In occupational hygiene, what does 'TLV-TWA' stand for in airborne chemical exposure limits?", "Threshold Limit Value - Time-Weighted Average (the average concentration for an 8-hour workday and 40-hour workweek without adverse effect)", ["Total Lethal Volume - Toxic Waste Allocation", "Threshold Limitation Velocity - Toxic Water Analysis", "Thermal Limit Variance - Technical Working Area"], "TLV-TWA represents the chemical exposure level to which nearly all workers may be repeatedly exposed day after day without health harm."),
    ("In chemical exposure toxicology, what is 'STEL'?", "Short-Term Exposure Limit (the maximum permissible concentration for a continuous 15-minute exposure period)", ["Standard Technical Emission Level", "Systemic Toxicity Evaluation Limit", "Safety Threshold for Environmental Liability"], "STEL prevents acute irritation, tissue damage, or narcosis from brief high spikes, even if the 8-hour TWA is within limits."),
    ("In chemical safety, what is 'IDLH' concentration?", "Immediately Dangerous to Life or Health (an airborne concentration that poses an immediate threat to life or irreversible impairment)", ["Internal Dosage Level for Healing", "Industrial Dust Level in Hazards", "International Drainage Limit of Hydrocarbons"], "IDLH environments mandate supplied-air respirators (SCBA); entry without positive-pressure breathing gear risks death within 30 minutes."),
    ("In confined space atmospheric testing, what is the mandatory sequence of gas testing before entry?", "1. Oxygen content, 2. Flammable gases and vapors (LEL), 3. Toxic contaminants (H2S, CO)", ["1. Toxic gases, 2. Oxygen, 3. Flammability", "1. Flammability, 2. Toxic gases, 3. Oxygen", "1. Temperature, 2. Humidity, 3. Dust"], "Oxygen must be tested first because catalytic LEL sensors require oxygen to function accurately, followed by combustible and toxic gases."),
    ("In flammability science, what is the difference between 'LFL' (Lower Flammable Limit) and 'UFL' (Upper Flammable Limit)?", "LFL is the leanest vapor-air mixture that will ignite; UFL is the richest mixture; between them lies the flammable/explosive range", ["LFL applies to solids; UFL applies to liquids", "LFL is measured in temperature; UFL is measured in pressure", "There is zero difference; both are identical numbers"], "Below LFL, the mixture is too lean to burn; above UFL, it is too rich (insufficient oxygen). Safety demands keeping vapors well below LFL."),
    ("In electrical safety, what is 'Arc Flash'?", "A catastrophic explosive release of thermal energy, intense light, and molten metal caused by an electrical arc short-circuit through air", ["A tiny static spark produced when taking off a woolen sweater", "The normal blue glow emitted by a computer laptop screen", "A lightning bolt striking a tall outdoor tree"], "Arc flash temperatures can exceed 35,000°F (hotter than the sun's surface), vaporizing copper and inflicting fatal fourth-degree burns."),
    ("In scaffolding safety, what is the '4:1 Rule' for leaning extension ladders?", "For every 4 feet of vertical ladder height, the ladder base should be positioned 1 foot away from the wall", ["Ladders must only be climbed by 4 workers simultaneously", "Ladders must be painted with 4 coats of safety yellow paint", "Ladders must be replaced every 4 calendar years"], "The 4:1 slope (approx. 75 degrees) ensures stability, preventing the ladder from tipping backward or sliding out at the base."),
    ("In working at heights safety, at what height is fall protection mandatorily required under general construction regulations?", "6 feet (1.8 meters) or more above a lower level", ["20 feet (6 meters)", "12 feet (3.6 meters)", "2 feet (0.6 meters)"], "Standard OSHA and Indian safety regulations mandate guardrails, safety nets, or personal fall arrest systems when working at heights of 1.8m+."),
    ("In fall arrest systems, what is the primary function of an 'Energy-Absorbing Lanyard' (Shock-Absorbing Lanyard)?", "To deploy internal tearing webbing that limits the maximum arrest force exerted on the human body to safe levels (< 8 kN / 1,800 lbs)", ["To pull the fallen worker back up to the roof automatically", "To alert emergency ambulance paramedics via GPS radio", "To prevent the safety harness from getting dirty"], "Without shock absorbers, the sudden kinetic arrest deceleration from a 6-foot fall can cause severe internal organ rupture and spine fractures."),
    ("In electrical lockout/tagout (LOTO), what is 'Zero Energy State' (Verification of Isolation)?", "Verifying that all residual or stored energy (electrical, pneumatic, hydraulic, kinetic, spring tension, thermal) has been fully dissipated", ["Turning off the room light switch before leaving", "Operating a factory without paying the monthly electric bill", "Disconnecting the battery of a handheld smartphone"], "LOTO is incomplete without testing: pushing start buttons, bleeding hydraulic pressure, and using voltmeters to confirm zero energy."),
    ("In accident investigation, what is the 'Swiss Cheese Model' of systemic accident causation (James Reason)?", "Catastrophes occur when holes (latent organizational defects and active failures) across multiple defensive barriers momentarily align", ["Accidents happen only when food is eaten near industrial machinery", "A financial audit method used by Swiss banking institutions", "A theory explaining why machine parts develop circular holes"], "Defense-in-depth establishes multiple protective barriers (engineering, training, maintenance); accidents breach all barriers simultaneously."),
    ("In the Swiss Cheese Model, what is an 'Active Failure' versus a 'Latent Condition'?", "Active failures are unsafe acts committed by frontline operators; Latent conditions are hidden systemic flaws in design, management, or culture", ["Active failures occur during the day; Latent conditions at night", "Active failures involve electricity; Latent conditions involve fire", "Active failures are intentional crimes; Latent conditions are accidents"], "Latent conditions (e.g., poor maintenance, understaffing, budget cuts) lie dormant for years until triggered by an active frontline error."),
    ("In industrial hygiene, what are the three primary physiological routes of chemical entry into the human body?", "Inhalation (breathing), Ingestion (swallowing), and Absorption / Inoculation through skin and mucous membranes", ["Hearing, Vision, and Smelling", "Touching, Walking, and Sleeping", "Speaking, Reading, and Writing"], "Inhalation is the most rapid and common route in workplace settings, delivering toxic vapors directly into blood via lung alveoli."),
    ("What is 'Silicosis' in occupational lung disease and which worker populations are most at risk?", "A progressive, irreversible fibrotic lung disease caused by inhaling crystalline silica dust (quarry, mining, stone crushing, sandblasting workers)", ["An acute bacterial infection treated with basic antibiotics", "A lung inflammation caused by drinking ice water", "A temporary cough caused by breathing cold winter fog"], "Silica particles lodge deep in lung tissue, triggering scarring and respiratory failure; prevented through wet suppression and N95/P100 respirators."),
    ("What is 'Asbestosis' and 'Mesothelioma' caused by in occupational health history?", "Chronic inhalation of airborne asbestos mineral fibers, causing severe lung fibrosis and malignant pleural cancer", ["Excessive exposure to loud industrial noise above 100 dB", "Skin contact with petroleum crude oil in refineries", "Working in air-conditioned office buildings without natural sunlight"], "Asbestos fibers are indestructible in tissue, with latency periods of 20 to 40 years between industrial exposure and fatal cancer diagnosis."),
    ("What is the primary difference between 'Heat Exhaustion' and 'Heat Stroke' in thermal workplace emergencies?", "Heat stroke is a medical emergency with body temperature > 104°F (40°C), hot dry skin, and altered mental status / coma; heat exhaustion features profuse sweating", ["Heat exhaustion requires an ambulance; Heat stroke requires rest", "Heat stroke only affects children; Heat exhaustion affects adults", "There is zero difference; both terms describe sunburns"], "Heat stroke shatters internal thermoregulation: sweat ceases, brain tissue cooks, and mortality is high without rapid immediate ice immersion."),
    ("What is the 'Rule of Nines' in burn injury medical triage and assessment?", "A clinical formula dividing adult body surface area into sections of roughly 9% (or multiples) to estimate total percentage burned", ["A rule stating that burns heal in exactly nine days", "A fire code mandating nine fire extinguishers per building", "A guideline requiring nine liters of water to quench a burn"], "Head = 9%, each Arm = 9%, Chest/Abdomen = 18%, Back = 18%, each Leg = 18%, Genitalia = 1%; guides emergency fluid resuscitation."),
    ("In workplace electrical safety, what is a 'GFCI' (Ground Fault Circuit Interrupter)?", "A fast-acting circuit breaker designed to shut off electric power in as little as 1/40th of a second when an imbalance of current is detected", ["A device that boosts electrical voltage to run heavy power tools", "A meter that records how much electricity a factory consumes", "A backup diesel generator that starts when power lines fail"], "GFCIs compare outgoing and returning current; a leakage of just 4 to 6 mA diverts into a human body triggers an instant trip, saving lives."),
    ("What is 'Lockout Hasps' in multi-operator LOTO procedures?", "A scissor-like hasp allowing multiple technicians to attach their individual personal padlocks to a single energy isolation switch", ["A heavy chain used to secure bicycles to outdoor railings", "A master key that can open any lock in the factory", "A magnetic sensor that detects when doors are left open"], "The machine cannot be re-energized until EVERY worker finishes their task and removes their individual padlock from the hasp."),
    ("In industrial ventilation engineering, what is 'Local Exhaust Ventilation' (LEV)?", "Capturing dusts, fumes, or toxic gases at their exact point of generation (hood/source) before they disperse into the general workspace", ["Opening all windows and doors to let fresh outdoor wind blow", "Installing high-powered ceiling ceiling fans in the cafeteria", "Using handheld paper fans to blow dust away from workbenches"], "LEV (hood, duct, filter/scrubber, fan) captures airborne hazards at high capture velocity at source, far superior to general dilution ventilation."),
    ("In hazardous chemical storage, what is the 'Segregation of Incompatible Chemicals' principle?", "Physically separating chemicals that react violently when mixed (e.g., storing acids separately from bases, and oxidizers away from flammables)", ["Storing chemicals alphabetically regardless of their hazard classes", "Mixing all leftover chemicals together in a single disposal drum", "Storing all liquid chemicals on high top shelves near ceilings"], "Mixing incompatibles (e.g., bleach and ammonia) produces lethal toxic chloramine gas; oxidizers in flammable storage fuel catastrophic infernos."),
    ("In radiation safety, what are the three fundamental cardinal principles of external radiation protection?", "Time (minimize exposure duration), Distance (maximize distance from source), and Shielding (use lead/concrete barriers)", ["Temperature, Pressure, and Density", "Air, Water, and Soil", "Electricity, Magnetism, and Gravity"], "Radiation dose drops with the square of the distance (Inverse Square Law); lead/concrete absorbs penetrating gamma and X-rays."),
    ("In industrial noise control, what is an 'Audiometric Testing' surveillance program?", "Periodic baseline and annual hearing tests conducted on noise-exposed workers to detect early Temporary or Permanent Threshold Shifts", ["Testing the maximum acoustic volume of factory fire alarms", "Testing the acoustic musical sound quality of company speakers", "Inspecting the exterior cleanliness of worker ears with torches"], "Audiometry detects high-frequency hearing loss (4,000 Hz notch) early, prompting re-engineering or upgraded hearing protection."),
    ("Under the Factories Act, 1948, when is an occupier legally required to report a fatal or serious workplace accident to the Inspector of Factories?", "Immediately, and within 48 hours in prescribed written statutory Form 18", ["Within 30 calendar days", "Only at the end of the corporate fiscal year", "Only if the injured worker files a formal police complaint"], "Section 88 mandates immediate telephonic notification and formal Form 18 submission for fatal accidents or injuries causing 48h+ disability."),
    ("What constitutes 'Reportable Accident' (Lost-Time Injury - LTI) under Indian labor safety laws?", "An injury that prevents the injured worker from resuming work for a period of 48 hours or more immediately following the accident", ["A minor paper cut treated with a small adhesive bandage", "An argument between two workers that ends in shouting", "A worker feeling tired and asking to go home 10 minutes early"], "An absence of 48+ hours triggers statutory reporting, lost-time injury frequency rate (LTIFR) calculations, and state inspections."),
    ("In ergonomics, what does 'RULA' stand for in posture assessment?", "Rapid Upper Limb Assessment (a ergonomic survey tool evaluating postural biomechanical risk to neck, trunk, and upper limbs)", ["Regional Urban Labor Administration", "Rotational Unit Load Alignment", "Repetitive Unit Lifting Assessment"], "RULA assigns numerical posture scores, categorizing risk from acceptable (Score 1-2) to urgent immediate investigation (Score 7)."),
    ("In first aid, what is the 'Recovery Position' used for?", "Placing an unconscious breathing casualty on their side with head tilted back to maintain an open airway and prevent choking on vomit", ["Laying a casualty flat on their stomach with face down", "Sitting a casualty upright on a chair with head bent forward", "Hanging an injured worker upside down by their ankles"], "The lateral recumbent recovery position uses gravitational drainage to ensure vomit or saliva drains freely without blocking lungs."),
    ("In emergency trauma response, what is a 'Tourniquet' used for?", "A tight constricting band applied around a limb to arrest catastrophic, life-threatening arterial hemorrhage when pressure fails", ["A soft cotton bandage wrapped around a sprained ankle", "A sling used to immobilize a broken forearm bone", "A splint used to straighten a dislocated finger"], "Modern windlass tourniquets applied high and tight stop lethal arterial spurting, preventing death from hemorrhagic shock within minutes."),
    ("In workplace toxicology, what is an 'Asphyxiant' and what is the difference between Simple and Chemical Asphyxiants?", "Simple asphyxiants displace oxygen physically (e.g., N2, CO2, Argon); Chemical asphyxiants block oxygen transport biochemically (e.g., CO, Cyanide)", ["Simple asphyxiants are liquids; Chemical asphyxiants are solids", "Simple asphyxiants cause burns; Chemical asphyxiants cause fractures", "There is zero difference; both terms mean the exact same thing"], "Nitrogen displacement causes silent death in seconds without gasping; Carbon Monoxide binds hemoglobin 200x tighter than oxygen."),
    ("What does 'PEL' stand for in workplace chemical safety standards?", "Permissible Exposure Limit (enforceable statutory regulatory limit set by government bodies like OSHA/DGMS)", ["Potential Environmental Liability", "Primary Emission Level", "Planned Evacuation Line"], "Unlike advisory guidelines (TLVs), PELs are legally binding limits; exceeding statutory PELs subjects employers to prosecution."),
    ("What is 'Job Safety Analysis' (JSA) or Job Hazard Analysis (JHA)?", "A systematic technique breaking a job into sequential steps, identifying hazards at each step, and defining specific safety controls", ["A psychological test evaluating if a worker likes their job", "A performance review determining annual salary increments", "A financial audit calculating how much revenue a job generates"], "JSAs engage frontline workers to create safe standard operating procedures before high-risk non-routine work begins."),
    ("In construction safety, what is a 'Toe Board' installed on scaffolding platforms?", "A vertical protective barrier (at least 4 inches high) installed along the platform edge to prevent tools and materials from falling off", ["A footrest installed for workers to sit comfortably during lunch", "A step-stool used to reach higher scaffolding pipe clamps", "A wooden plank used to clean muddy boots before climbing"], "Toe boards stop kicked tools, bolts, and bricks from tumbling over platform edges onto personnel working below."),
    ("What does 'OSHA' stand for in global occupational health and safety standards?", "Occupational Safety and Health Administration", ["Operational Sanitation and Health Agency", "Office of Systemic Hazard Assessment", "Organization of Security and Hazard Auditing"], "OSHA (US Department of Labor) sets and enforces protective workplace health and safety standards that serve as international benchmarks.")
]

hs_hard = [
    ("In fire protection engineering, what is the mathematical formula for the water flow rate discharge from a fire sprinkler nozzle?", "Q = K * sqrt(P) (where Q is flow rate in GPM, K is sprinkler discharge coefficient, and P is operating pressure in PSI)", ["Q = K * P^2", "Q = (K * P) / 2", "Q = K / sqrt(P)"], "Sprinkler hydraulic calculations use the K-factor formula to ensure sufficient water density (GPM/sq ft) to suppress design fires."),
    ("In blast explosion physics, what is the difference between a 'Deflagration' and a 'Detonation'?", "Deflagration propagates at subsonic speeds via thermal conduction (< 343 m/s); Detonation propagates at supersonic speeds via shock waves (> 1,000 m/s)", ["Deflagration produces fire; Detonation produces only smoke", "Deflagration occurs in solids; Detonation occurs only in liquids", "Deflagration is caused by electricity; Detonation by chemicals"], "Deflagrations generate high pressure inside enclosures; detonations produce destructive, shattering supersonic shock fronts (brisance)."),
    ("In industrial hygiene, how is the composite 8-hour Time-Weighted Average (TWA) calculated for variable multi-period exposures?", "TWA = (C1*T1 + C2*T2 + ... + Cn*Tn) / Total Shift Time (where C is concentration and T is duration in hours)", ["TWA = (C1 + C2 + ... + Cn) / n", "TWA = Maximum Concentration recorded during the shift", "TWA = (Highest C - Lowest C) / 2"], "TWA integrates changing airborne exposure levels throughout a shift; the cumulative product sum cannot exceed statutory exposure limits."),
    ("In chemical toxicology, what is the 'Additive Mixture Rule' for simultaneous exposure to multiple hazardous air contaminants?", "Hazard Index = (C1/L1) + (C2/L2) + ... + (Cn/Ln); if Hazard Index > 1.0, the threshold limit is exceeded", ["Hazard Index = (C1 + C2 + Cn) / (L1 + L2 + Ln)", "Hazard Index = C_max / L_min", "Hazard Index = (C1 * C2) / (L1 * L2)"], "When airborne chemicals affect the same target organ system (e.g., liver or central nervous system), their fractional exposures are additive."),
    ("In electrical safety engineering, what does 'Dalziel's Formula' (Charles Dalziel) calculate regarding electric shock ventricular fibrillation?", "I_fib = k / sqrt(t) (Fibrillation threshold current in mA based on body shock duration t in seconds, where k ~ 116 for 50kg humans)", ["I_fib = Voltage * Resistance", "I_fib = Power / Frequency", "I_fib = Voltage / (t^2)"], "Dalziel's research established international electrical safety standards, governing the trip sensitivity and tripping speed curves of GFCIs."),
    ("In fall protection mechanics, what is 'Fall Factor' and how is it calculated?", "Fall Factor = Free Fall Distance / Total Length of Lanyard (ranges from 0 to 2 in personal fall arrest systems)", ["Fall Factor = Worker Body Weight / Anchor Point Strength", "Fall Factor = Height of Building / Ladder Length", "Fall Factor = Wind Velocity / Platform Width"], "Fall Factor 2 (anchor at feet) generates the maximum possible deceleration shock load on the body; anchors should always be overhead (Factor 0)."),
    ("In respiratory protective equipment (RPE), what is 'Assigned Protection Factor' (APF)?", "The workplace level of respiratory protection that a properly functioning, correctly fitted respirator is expected to provide to wearers", ["The financial price markup charged by safety equipment vendors", "The percentage of oxygen present in compressed air cylinders", "The total weight of the respirator mask measured in grams"], "APF = Ambient Contaminant Concentration / Concentration inside mask. Half-face masks have APF 10; full-face APF 50; SCBA APF 10,000."),
    ("If an industrial workshop has an airborne lead dust concentration of 0.5 mg/m3 and the PEL is 0.05 mg/m3, what minimum APF respirator is required?", "APF = 10 (0.5 / 0.05 = 10; a half-mask with APF 10 or full-face mask with APF 50 is required)", ["APF = 2", "APF = 100", "APF = 1,000"], "The required minimum APF is calculated by dividing ambient contaminant concentration by the occupational exposure limit (PEL)."),
    ("In quantitative fit testing (QNFT) of respirators, what does the 'Fit Factor' measure?", "The ratio of ambient aerosol concentration outside the respirator mask to the concentration that leaks inside the mask during testing", ["The physical hat size of the worker's head in inches", "The volume of air inhaled by the lungs per breath", "The maximum air pressure delivered by the compressor"], "A fit factor of at least 100 is required for tight-fitting half-face masks, and 500 for full-face masks to verify an effective face seal."),
    ("In occupational noise engineering, what is the '3 dB Exchange Rate' (Equal Energy Hypothesis - ISO/NIOSH)?", "Every 3 dB increase in sound pressure level doubles the acoustic sound energy, cutting the maximum safe permissible exposure time in half", ["Every 3 dB increase requires wearing three pairs of earplugs", "Noise doubles in volume every 3 meters of distance", "Hearing loss occurs only when noise exceeds 300 dB"], "Under the 3 dB rule: 85 dBA is safe for 8 hours; 88 dBA is safe for only 4 hours; 91 dBA for 2 hours; 100 dBA for only 15 minutes."),
    ("In radiation safety physics, what is the 'Inverse Square Law' for point sources of ionizing radiation?", "Radiation Intensity is inversely proportional to the square of the distance from the source (I1 / I2 = d2^2 / d1^2)", ["Radiation Intensity doubles as distance doubles", "Radiation Intensity drops linearly with distance", "Radiation Intensity is completely independent of distance"], "Doubling the distance from a gamma radiation source drops exposure to 1/4th (25%); tripling distance cuts exposure down to 1/9th (11%)."),
    ("In radiation biology, what is the difference between 'Absorbed Dose' (Gray / Rad) and 'Equivalent Dose' (Sievert / Rem)?", "Absorbed Dose measures physical energy deposited per unit mass (Gy); Equivalent Dose weights absorbed dose by radiation type (Sv) for biological damage", ["Gray is for gamma rays; Sievert is for sound waves", "Absorbed dose is for water; Equivalent dose is for air", "There is zero difference; both are identical units"], "Equivalent Dose (Sv) = Absorbed Dose (Gy) * Radiation Weighting Factor (w_R); alpha particles have w_R = 20, inflicting 20x biological cell damage."),
    ("In industrial hygiene thermodynamics, what is the 'WBGT' (Wet Bulb Globe Temperature) index?", "A composite environmental heat stress index combining Natural Wet Bulb temperature, Globe temperature (radiant heat), and Dry Bulb temperature", ["A thermometer tracking the temperature of boiling water boilers", "A weather forecast predicting regional winter frost freezes", "An instrument measuring the temperature of human skin sweat"], "Outdoor WBGT = 0.7*NWB + 0.2*GT + 0.1*DB. It sets work-rest cycles to prevent fatal exertional heat stroke in heavy industries."),
    ("In hazardous area classification (ATEX / NEC), what is a 'Zone 0' (Class I, Division 1) hazardous area?", "An explosive atmosphere consisting of a mixture with air of flammable substances is present continuously or for long periods (> 1,000 hrs/year)", ["An explosive atmosphere is likely to occur only during rare maintenance", "An explosive atmosphere never occurs under normal conditions", "An administrative office area with standard air conditioning"], "Zone 0 (e.g., vapor space inside fuel storage tanks) mandates intrinsically safe equipment ('Ex ia') incapable of causing sparks under faults."),
    ("In explosion protection engineering, what is 'Intrinsic Safety' (Ex 'i')?", "An electrical protection technique limiting the electrical and thermal energy of circuits so they cannot ignite a specific hazardous gas mixture", ["Enclosing electrical circuits inside heavy explosion-proof steel boxes", "Purging motor enclosures continuously with compressed air", "Filling electrical transformer tanks with sand or oil"], "Intrinsically safe circuits restrict voltage, current, capacitance, and inductance so sparks or hot surfaces lack sufficient energy to trigger ignition."),
    ("In process safety management (PSM), what is a 'Safety Integrity Level' (SIL) under IEC 61508 / IEC 61511?", "A discrete tier (SIL 1 to SIL 4) specifying the required safety instrumented performance to achieve target Probability of Failure on Demand (PFD)", ["The salary grade assigned to licensed safety inspectors", "The number of safety drills conducted in a chemical plant per year", "The physical thickness of steel pressure vessel walls"], "SIL 4 represents the highest risk reduction tier (PFD 10^-5 to 10^-4), reserved for catastrophic nuclear or chemical process hazards."),
    ("In process safety management (PSM), what is 'Management of Change' (MOC)?", "A formal multidisciplinary review and authorization procedure before implementing any change in process chemicals, technology, equipment, or procedures", ["Replacing the corporate chief executive officer with a new director", "Promoting a factory worker to safety supervisor", "Changing the brand of coffee served in the company cafeteria"], "Catastrophes (e.g., Flixborough, Bhopal) often result from unreviewed 'temporary' engineering bypasses or minor unvetted process alterations."),
    ("In industrial chemical toxicology, what is 'IDLH' of Hydrogen Sulfide (H2S), a notorious sewer gas?", "100 ppm (H2S deadens the olfactory sense of smell rapidly at 100-150 ppm, causing instant olfactory paralysis before sudden respiratory arrest)", ["1,000 ppm", "10 ppm", "5,000 ppm"], "H2S is treacherous: relying on smell is fatal because high concentrations paralyze the olfactory nerve immediately, causing collapse ('knockdown')."),
    ("In ergonomics and biomechanics, what does the 'NIOSH Lifting Equation' calculate?", "The Recommended Weight Limit (RWL) for a manual lifting task based on load constant (51 lbs / 23 kg) multiplied by six task reduction multipliers", ["The maximum weight a commercial transport truck can carry on highways", "The total weight of exercise barbells used in physical fitness gyms", "The tensile breaking strength of steel crane hoist cables"], "RWL = LC * HM * VM * DM * AM * FM * CM. Lifting Index (LI = Actual Weight / RWL) > 1.0 indicates elevated risk of lower back disc injury."),
    ("In ergonomics, what does 'Cumulative Trauma Disorder' (CTD) or 'Repetitive Strain Injury' (RSI) stem from?", "Chronic micro-trauma to soft tissues (muscles, tendons, nerves) caused by repetitive motions, forceful exertion, awkward postures, and inadequate rest", ["A single sudden catastrophic bone fracture from a high fall", "A bacterial infection caught from dirty drinking water", "A chemical burn suffered from an accidental acid splash"], "CTDs (e.g., tendonitis, carpal tunnel, epicondylitis) develop gradually; prevented by task rotation, ergonomic redesign, and frequent micro-breaks."),
    ("In occupational cancer epidemiology, which heavy metal is historically linked to 'Nasal Sinus and Lung Cancer' in nickel refineries?", "Inhaled Nickel subsulfide and Nickel oxide refinery dusts (Group 1 human carcinogens)", ["Inorganic metallic sodium salts", "Calcium carbonate limestone dust", "Pure iron oxide rust particles"], "IARC classifies nickel refining dusts as Group 1 carcinogens, mandating ultra-low occupational exposure limits and closed automated refining."),
    ("In industrial hygiene, what is a 'Cyclone Sampler' used for in aerosol particle sampling?", "Separating and collecting the respirable fraction of airborne dust (particles < 4-10 microns that penetrate deep into lung alveoli)", ["Measuring the wind velocity of tropical cyclone storms", "Collecting large physical chunks of gravel from roadbeds", "Filtering municipal drinking water using carbon filters"], "Centrifugal acceleration deposits coarse non-respirable particles in the grit pot, allowing only alveolar-penetrating dust onto the filter cassette."),
    ("In safety performance metrics, what is 'Lost Time Injury Frequency Rate' (LTIFR) mathematically calculated as?", "LTIFR = (Number of Lost Time Injuries * 1,000,000) / Total Man-Hours Worked", ["LTIFR = Total Injuries / Total Number of Employees", "LTIFR = (Lost Days * 100) / Total Payroll Cost", "LTIFR = Number of Near Misses / Number of Accidents"], "LTIFR standardizes safety performance per million work hours, allowing objective benchmarking across corporate divisions and industries."),
    ("In safety metrics, what is 'Lost Time Injury Severity Rate' (LTISR)?", "LTISR = (Total Days Lost to Lost Time Injuries * 1,000,000) / Total Man-Hours Worked", ["LTISR = Total Injuries / Total Lost Days", "LTISR = Number of Fatalities * 100", "LTISR = Medical Bills Paid / Total Man-Hours"], "While frequency rate (LTIFR) measures how often accidents happen, severity rate (LTISR) measures the catastrophic duration and severity of harm."),
    ("In behavioral safety science, what is 'Behavior-Based Safety' (BBS)?", "A systematic peer observation and feedback process focusing on workers' observable actions to reinforce safe behaviors and eliminate at-risk habits", ["Punishing workers with financial salary deductions for every minor error", "Relying strictly on automated computer surveillance cameras", "Banning employees from speaking to each other while working"], "BBS engages frontline employees as safety observers, collecting data on behavioral patterns and addressing systemic barriers to safe behavior."),
    ("What does 'Safety Culture' versus 'Safety Climate' distinguish in organizational sociology?", "Safety Culture represents deeply held shared values, beliefs, and enduring norms; Safety Climate represents current shared perceptions of safety priorities", ["Safety culture is for construction; Safety climate is for weather forecasting", "Safety culture applies to managers; Safety climate applies to workers", "There is zero difference; both terms are interchangeable"], "Safety climate is a snapshot of current employee perceptions; safety culture is the deep-seated institutional character that endures over time."),
    ("In construction safety, what is 'Soil Mechanics: Trench Cave-in Protection' mandate for trenches deeper than 5 feet (1.5 meters)?", "Sloping (benching), Shoring (hydraulic/timber supports), or Shielding (trench boxes) are legally mandatory to prevent fatal burial", ["Workers must wear running shoes to run out quickly if soil collapses", "Digging trenches only during daylight hours without rain", "Filling trenches with loose sand before workers enter"], "One cubic yard of soil weighs nearly 3,000 lbs (equivalent to an automobile); trench collapses crush lungs instantly, making cave-in shields mandatory."),
    ("In electrical transmission lines, what is 'Step Potential' versus 'Touch Potential' during a ground fault?", "Step potential is voltage between feet when walking near fault; Touch potential is voltage between hand touching energized structure and feet on ground", ["Step potential is for ladders; Touch potential is for switches", "Step potential is AC voltage; Touch potential is DC voltage", "Step potential is low; Touch potential is zero"], "When a high-voltage wire hits the ground, voltage dissipates radially; walking creates a lethal voltage drop between feet (hop or shuffle to escape)."),
    ("In emergency chemical spills, what does 'ERG' stand for in transportation dangerous goods response?", "Emergency Response Guidebook (published by US DOT, Transport Canada, and SCT Mexico for first responders during transport incidents)", ["Emergency Radiation Guidelines", "Environmental Remediation Group", "Explosion Resistance Guide"], "The ERG links 4-digit UN chemical identification numbers (e.g., UN 1075 for LPG) to immediate hazard zones, isolation distances, and fire fighting tactics."),
    ("In industrial ventilation, what is 'Capture Velocity'?", "The air velocity required at any point in front of a ventilation hood to overcome opposing air currents and draw contaminants into the hood", ["The speed at which a drone captures aerial video footage", "The velocity of water flowing through sprinkler pipelines", "The speed at which delivery trucks drive into warehouses"], "Capture velocities range from 50-100 FPM for quiet vapors up to 500-2,000 FPM for grinding dust, ensuring toxic particulates do not escape."),
    ("In laser safety (ANSI Z136), what is 'Maximum Permissible Exposure' (MPE)?", "The level of laser radiation to which a person may be exposed without hazardous biological effects or adverse eye or skin damage", ["The highest wattage of laser used in stage concerts", "The maximum distance a laser beam can travel through air", "The speed at which laser light travels through vacuum"], "Class 3B and Class 4 lasers exceed MPE in fractions of a second, mandating wavelength-specific optical density (OD) protective eyewear."),
    ("In hyperbaric and diving occupational safety, what causes 'Decompression Sickness' (The Bends)?", "Rapid ambient pressure reduction causing dissolved inert Nitrogen gas in tissues to come out of solution and form mechanical bubbles in blood and joints", ["Drinking ice-cold water immediately after deep breathing", "Breathing pure oxygen at atmospheric ground pressure", "Holding one's breath while swimming in shallow pools"], "Prevented by controlled decompression stops and hyperbaric recompression chambers; bubbles block capillary blood flow and cause excruciating pain."),
    ("In hazardous waste site cleanup operations, what are the four levels of EPA PPE Protection (Level A, B, C, D)?", "Level A: fully encapsulating vapor-protective suit with SCBA; Level B: splash suit with SCBA; Level C: splash suit with APR respirator; Level D: work uniform", ["Level A: cotton shirt; Level B: raincoat; Level C: boots; Level D: helmet", "Level A: gloves; Level B: goggles; Level C: earplugs; Level D: vest", "Level A: indoor; Level B: outdoor; Level C: underground; Level D: underwater"], "Level A offers maximum skin, eye, and respiratory defense against unknown or lethal toxic chemical vapors and toxic gases.")
]
